From 64b66541d30121f7252bdbfe4720741003607967 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 13:45:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(rotation): rotate the figures we own, at a size where
 they can be read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two archives were not two versions — settled again, this time visually.
part1/part2 is 01/02 at 1600x1074 instead of 1168x784: same room, same callout
plates in the same positions, same five species with the same Janka values. A
crossfade between an image and a softer copy of itself reads as a page failing
to load. There was nothing to rotate.

Rotating the 74 behind the hero would also have been wrong, and it is worth
saying why so nobody tries it later: most carry callout labels, and a labelled
figure under a 0.55 scrim with a headline over it is noise. It degrades the hero
and wastes the figure at once.

So FigureRotator shows them at readable size with their captions and a link to
where each is explained — on the homepage as "The Science: what we can explain",
eight figures running moisture through movement, failure, method and cost, and
on /resources as "Shown, not listed". Someone who came for a price now sees in
twenty seconds that this company can explain moisture differential, the
four-machine sequence and what cupping is, without clicking anything.

Every slide is in the DOM at all times and only opacity changes, so a crawler
reads all eight captions whether or not it runs the timer. The stage reserves
the manifest's aspect ratio so nothing shifts. The first slide is server-
rendered and the only one marked priority. Auto-advance stops on hover, on
focus, on a hidden tab, and entirely under prefers-reduced-motion — checked in
the component, because a setInterval is not an animation and the stylesheet's
global reset cannot see it.

The pool guard is the part worth reading. Pools drop unresolvable ids silently,
which is right at runtime and unacceptable unchecked, so the ids are verified
against the manifest. The first version was tested by injecting
'concept-mc-differentialX' and it PASSED — the extractor read [a-z0-9-]+ and a
capital letter meant the string never matched, so the guard never saw the id it
was meant to reject. F-117, F-149, F-166 and F-177 in a new costume. It now
reads any quoted string, and both a capitalised typo and a plausible lowercase
rename are proven to fail the build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
